"9.2. Properties of Arcs www.ck12.org 9.2 Properties of Arcs Learning Objectives • Deﬁne and measure central angles in circles. • Deﬁne minor arcs and major arcs. Review Queue 1. What kind of triangle is △ABC? 2. How does BD relate to △ABC? 3. Find m̸ ABC and m̸ ABD. Round to the nearest tenth. 4. Find AD. 5. Find AC. Know What? The Ferris wheel to the right has equally spaced seats, such that the central angle is 20◦. How many seats are there? Why do you think it is important to have equally spaced seats on a Ferris wheel?"
"www.ck12.org Circles If the radius of this Ferris wheel is 25 ft., how far apart are two adjacent seats? Round your answer to the nearest tenth. The shortest distance between two points is a straight line. Central Angles & Arcs Central Angle: The angle formed by two radii of the circle with its vertex at the center of the circle. In the picture to the right, the central angle would be ̸ BAC. Every central angle divides a circle into two arcs. In this case the arcs are c BC and d BDC. Notice the J above the letters. To label an arc, always use this curve above the letters. Do not confuse BC and c BC. Arc: A section of the circle. If D was not on the circle, we would not be able to tell the difference between c BC and d BDC. There are 360◦in a circle, where a semicircle is half of a circle, or 180◦. m̸ EFG = 180◦, because it is a straight angle, so m [ EHG = 180◦ and m d EJG = 180◦. Semicircle: An arc that measures 180◦."
"9.2. Properties of Arcs www.ck12.org Minor Arc: An arc that is less than 180◦. Major Arc: An arc that is greater than 180◦. Always use 3 letters to label a major arc. An arc can be measured in degrees or in a linear measure (cm, ft, etc.). In this chapter we will use degree measure. The measure of the minor arc is the same as the measure of the central angle that corresponds to it. The measure of the major arc equals to 360◦minus the measure of the minor arc. In order to prevent confusion, major arcs are always named with three letters; the letters that denote the endpoints of the arc and any other point on the major arc. When referring to the measure of an arc, always place an “m” in from of the label. Example 1: Find mc AB and m d ADB in JC. Solution: mc AB is the same as m̸ ACB. So, mc AB = 102◦. The measure of m d ADB, which is the major arc, is equal to 360◦minus the minor arc. m d ADB = 360◦−mc AB = 360◦−102◦= 258◦ Example 2: Find the measures of the arcs in JA. EB is a diameter. Solution: Because EB is a diameter, m̸ EAB = 180◦. Each arc is the same as its corresponding central angle. m c BF = m̸ FAB = 60◦ m c EF = m̸ EAF = 120◦ →m̸ EAB−m̸ FAB m c ED = m̸ EAD = 38◦ →m̸ EAB−m̸ BAC −m̸ CAD m c DC = m̸ DAC = 90◦ mc BC = m̸ BAC = 52◦ Congruent Arcs: Two arcs are congruent if their central angles are congruent. Example 3: List all the congruent arcs in JC below. AB and DE are diameters."
"www.ck12.org Circles Solution: From the picture, we see that ̸ ACD and ̸ ECB are vertical angles. ̸ DCB and ̸ ACE are also vertical angles. Because all vertical angles are equal and these four angles are all central angles, we know that c AD ∼= c EB and c AE ∼= c DB. Example 4: Are the blue arcs congruent? Explain why or why not. a) b) Solution: In part a, c AD ∼= c BC because they have the same central angle measure. In part b, the two arcs do have the same measure, but are not congruent because the circles are not congruent. Arc Addition Postulate Just like the Angle Addition Postulate and the Segment Addition Postulate, there is an Arc Addition Postulate. It is very similar. Arc Addition Postulate: The measure of the arc formed by two adjacent arcs is the sum of the measures of the two arcs. Using the picture from Example 3, we would say mc AE +mc EB = m d AEB. Example 5: Reusing the ﬁgure from Example 2, ﬁnd the measure of the following arcs in JA. EB is a diameter."
"9.2. Properties of Arcs www.ck12.org a) m[ FED b) m[ CDF c) m c BD d) m[ DFC Solution: Use the Arc Addition Postulate. a) m[ FED = m c FE +m c ED = 120◦+38◦= 158◦ We could have labeled [ FED as c FD because it is less than 180◦. b) m[ CDF = m c CD+m c DE +m c EF = 90◦+38◦+120◦= 248◦ c) m c BD = mc BC +m c CD = 52◦+90◦= 142◦ d) m[ DFC = 38◦+120◦+60◦+52◦= 270◦or m[ DFC = 360◦−m c CD = 360◦−90◦= 270◦ Example 6: Algebra Connection Find the value of x for JC below. Solution: There are 360◦in a circle. Let’s set up an equation. mc AB+m c AD+m c DB = 360◦ (4x+15)◦+92◦+(6x+3)◦= 360◦ 10x+110◦= 360◦ 10x = 250◦ x = 25◦ Know What? Revisited Because the seats are 20◦apart, there will be 360◦ 20◦= 18 seats. It is important to have the seats evenly spaced for balance. To determine how far apart the adjacent seats are, use the triangle to the right. We will need to use sine to ﬁnd x and then multiply it by 2."
